  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 935 PM MDT Tue Jun 30 2026\n\nThere is little change in the longwave pattern through the end of \nthe week, as troughing dominates the west and ridging to the east. \nThis keeps eastern Utah and western Colorado under breezy \nsouthwesterly flow. Afternoon gusts of 20-30 mph are expected daily. \nPassing shortwaves will bring periods of higher wind gusts, 25-35 \nmph, with locally up to 40 mph. Additionally, these waves will have \njust enough moisture with them to create a few virga showers or dry \nthunderstorms over northeastern Utah, raising the concern for \nlightning starts. Surface levels remain extremely dry, with daytime \nminimum humidity values in the single digits for the lower \nelevations, and the low teens higher up. As a result, Critical Fire \nWeather conditions remain with us through at least the end of the \nweek. Red Flag Warnings are in effect from noon to 9PM Wednesday, \nwith Fire Weather Watches in effect from noon to 9PM Thursday. \n\nThe slightly cooler airmass associated with the trough will keep our \ntemperatures at or a few degrees below normal through the end of the \nweek. However, by the weekend flow aloft begins to shift zonal as \nhigh pressure tries to build into the southwest. Winds will finally \nrelax, and temperatures will jump to around 5 degrees above normal \nin response. There are hints that some subtropical moisture will \nwork its way northward under the high by early next week, however \nthe overall pattern isn't that favorable for a good tap of deep \nmoisture. With the high directly over the Desert Southwest, moisture \nis more likely to reach areas to our east and west. And with dry \nsurface conditions still in place, virga showers, gusty winds, and \ndry thunderstorms are more likely that any substantial wetting\nrain.",
